==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|sphincter}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|sphincter}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|σφιγκτήρ||band, contractile muscle}}, from {{m|grc|σφίγγω||I bind tight}}. Compare [[sphinx]], "the strangler".

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈsfɪŋktə/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} A [[ringlike]] [[band]] of [[muscle]] that surrounds a bodily opening, [[constrict]]ing and [[relax]]ing as required for normal [[physiological]] functioning.
#: ''the '''sphincter''' of the bladder''
#: ''the iris '''sphincter''' in the eye''
#* '''1982''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''Constance'' (Faber & Faber, 2004: ''Avignon Quintet''), page 836:
#*: She decided that she would force him to climax first by the sheer strength of her young animal control, the strength of her '''sphincters''' {{...}}
## {{lb|en|in particular}} The internal or external anal sphincter, to the extent these are distinguished as two separate entities.

